    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d824">

                <pb facs="1114" n="1100" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1100"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15250</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2038</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-18">January 18,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received February 1.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the Embassy’s
                    telegrams nos. 97, January 15, 11 a.m. and 98, January 15, 1 p.m., on the above
                    subject, and to enclose<anchor n="9" xml:id="d824-f1"/> a copy of the Embassy’s
                    note of January 14 to the Foreign Office, together with a copy of a memorandum
                    of conversation of January 15 between the Political Vice Minister for Foreign
                        Affairs<anchor n="10" xml:id="d824-f2"/> and the Counselor of this
                        Embassy.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d824-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>It will be noted from the memorandum that it was not possible for me to see the
                    Foreign Minister as Dr. Soong was out of town; that Dr. Wu exhibited a feeling
                    of considerable regret over the occurrence of another incident of this nature;
                    and that he said that the Embassy’s <pb facs="1115" n="1101" xml:id="pg1101"/>
                    note would be brought expeditiously to the Generalissimo<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d824-f4"/> and that the Generalissimo would give the closest
                    attention to the matter.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d824-f1" n="9" xml:id="d824-f1-content">
